WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
- Hindsight is of course 20/20.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.640
But now having practiced
law for over 10 years,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.320
there is so much that I
would have done differently

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.520
in law school if I had
known then what I know now.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.170
So here's a list of things
I would've done differently

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.630
if I had to do law school all over again.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.508
Please, learn from my mistakes.

00:00:17.508 --> 00:00:21.175
(plucky instrumental music)

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.850
Okay, so what would I do differently

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.070
if I had to do law school all over again?

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:28.680
Well, the first thing that I would do

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.113
is I would spend way less
time preparing for class.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.530
In law school, class participation

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.270
usually counts for almost nothing.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.670
At best it counts for maybe
five or 10% of your grade.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.500
There's almost no class participation.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.030
Preparing for class is a time-suck.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.640
It doesn't help you on the final exam.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.180
Your job is not to understand everything

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.200
by the time you get to class.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.410
If your professor's doing their job,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.630
they will help you understand
it when you get to class.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.850
I know the idea of getting cold-called

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.980
in class is so scary, and
nobody wants to look dumb

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.400
in front of their classmates,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.830
but it will not matter how
hard you prepare for class,

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.430
even if you do get called on.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:13.460
The professor creates
their questions in a way

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.940
that no one could possibly
answer all of them.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.220
If you do the reading,
and preferably you use

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.430
our flipped-case method, the
link for that is down below,

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.800
then you'll be prepared enough for class.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.650
But you should prepare for
class in an efficient way.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:32.330
Don't obsess about getting
everything perfect beforehand.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:35.570
Understand that by the time
you start studying for finals,

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.040
you will have forgotten everything

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.570
that you did to prepare for class.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.100
I would have put in the absolute minimum

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.270
to prepare for class and
would've used class time

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.400
to refine my thinking
and work on my outline.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.460
I would've taken the time I used

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.930
to spend preparing for class,

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.490
and I would've started
preparing for finals sooner

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.170
because it's the finals
where you get your grade,

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:59.130
not preparing for class.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.370
So, in retrospect, I would've
spent so much less time

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:06.270
obsessing over every nuance
and minutiae in the case

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.370
and preparing for class on the odd chance

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.260
that I got called on by the professor,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:13.750
and I just would have
prepared for finals instead.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:18.140
Also, even if your class does
have participation points,

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.614
you don't have to rely
on the only interaction

00:02:20.614 --> 00:02:23.200
of your professor being
when you're cold-called.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.540
You should ask questions of your professor

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.000
when you have legitimate questions.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.470
And on top of that, you
should go to office hours

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:33.240
and talk to your professor
in a less formal environment.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.460
These are all ways that you can

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.140
raise your participation grade

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.580
while also bettering your understanding

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.260
of the case material itself.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.800
The next that I would've done differently

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.040
is I would've started taking
practice tests way earlier.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.710
We talk about this a lot at Legal Eagle.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.150
Practice tests are one of
the most important things

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.720
that you can do to get
better grades in law school.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.100
Writing a law school exam is

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.400
unlike any other kind of writing.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.900
That's why English majors have

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.980
such a hard time with law school.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.700
They, more than anyone else,

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.910
have to undo the habits that
they learned in college.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.950
A lotta the things that they
learn in terms of good writing

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.200
are actually bad for writing quickly

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.670
and clearly on a law school exam.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:17.170
In fact, that's why at Legal
Eagle we created a master class

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.330
specifically teaching people
how to think like a lawyer

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.783
and write like a lawyer on a final exam.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.800
I would have started taking practice tests

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.430
at least halfway through
the semester if not sooner.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:31.180
I know that sounds crazy,
but it is never too early

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.090
to start taking practice tests,

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:36.200
and it is guaranteed to
get you better grades.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:37.590
The next thing I would've
done in law school

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:38.980
is I would've taken a course

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:42.370
on how to write law school
final exams earlier.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.790
Because law school final
exams are so unusual

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:48.570
and because your law school
won't teach you the skill,

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.470
it is so important to learn what

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:53.440
a law school final exam looks like

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.653
and how you're supposed to write them.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.900
Odds are if you took the LSAT,

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.250
you probably took some courses

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:02.430
on how to answer the questions,

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.480
whether it is a logic game
or reading comprehension.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.320
There's so much to learn
that's specific to the LSAT

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.010
that you probably had no frame
of reference for in college.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.800
The LSAT is not like the SAT,

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.860
and it's certainly not
like your college classes.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.020
And it's the same way when
you get to law school itself.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:23.400
Law school final exams
are unlike any other test

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.350
in any other field at any other

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.520
graduate or professional school.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.220
They are issue-spotting exams

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.810
where you have to actually
use the law itself.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.740
Just showing the professor that you

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.290
know the law is not enough;
you have to apply it.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:40.650
There is a method and a science

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.860
to breaking down issue-spotting essays

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.870
and writing an A exam every single time,

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.960
and it has very little to do

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.340
with how much law you actually know.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.930
The person that knows less law

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.110
but more about how to write the essay

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.190
is gonna get a better grade

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.510
than the person that knows more of the law

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:01.390
and memorized more of the
facts in class discussion

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.970
but doesn't actually know
how to spot the issues

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.960
and how to write them
down on the final exam.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.100
Like the LSAT, answering law school

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.290
issue spotters is a skill,

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:14.110
and you can get better at any skill.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:17.400
You can also learn how to
do that particular skill.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.540
There's strategies that you can
use to get better over time.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:23.230
And the better you get at final exams,

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:25.520
the better your grades will
be at the end of the year.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.080
The next tip, the next
thing I would have done

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.410
differently in law school
is I would've lived

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.360
much, much closer to campus.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.670
When I went to law school,

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:35.450
I actually didn't live that far away,

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:38.220
but the law school was
on one end of the campus,

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:39.930
and I lived on the other end of campus.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.820
So it took me probably about 45 minutes

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:45.410
to walk each way to and from class

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:48.630
because it was a very large campus.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.960
So even though I was
within walking distance

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.120
of the law school, it still
took 45 minutes each way,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.980
or an hour and 1/2 each day out of my day,

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.440
to walk to and from class, and that's only

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:02.950
if I went to the law school once a day.

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:05.600
If I went twice, then that's
three hours that I lost.

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.130
It's even worse for the people

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.950
that have to commute to school.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.840
During that commuting
time, you can't study.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:14.800
It's just dead time, and worse,

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.760
it's probably stressing you out.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.570
Driving is no fun when
you're on a deadline.

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:21.300
One of the worst things that you can do

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.140
is live so far away from
campus that you have to drive.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.580
Commuting is just the worst.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:29.710
It's time that you can't study.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:31.490
It's probably pretty stressful

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.210
because driving on a deadline

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.320
just tends to be very stressful.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.690
And it's probably exhausting too.

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:39.600
So if you're commuting to class,

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.250
that is hours a day where you can't study.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:43.850
And it's just making you more tired,

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:44.960
so it's going to prevent you

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:47.033
from studying later on in the day.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.130
Often, if you live very close to campus,

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:51.720
there's no doubt that the rent costs

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.810
are going to be more,
but at the same time,

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:56.530
you can probably forego
the cost of owning a car,

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:59.230
having to insure it, having to pay gas.

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:01.270
So even though the rent might be more,

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:03.810
it might actually save
you money in the long run.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.920
And frankly, if you're going
to law school full-time,

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:08.460
you're probably already on the hook

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.030
for spending at least $100,000.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.350
So spending slightly more to avoid

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.290
commuting is completely worth it,

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.240
especially if you can turn the time

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.530
that you would otherwise commute to class

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.570
into studying time where
you can get better grades,

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:24.610
that will result in a better,

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.090
higher-paying job down the line.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.110
That's what law school's all about anyway.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:29.690
So, if I had to it all over again,

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:32.880
I would've lived as close to
the law school as possible

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:36.240
so that I could just get
up, walk across the street,

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.260
and then go to law school
without even thinking about it.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:39.790
I would've saved so much time,

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:41.840
I would've been far less tired,

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:44.720
and I would've been able
to study more and better.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.160
The next thing that I
would've done differently,

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.240
I would have trusted
the professor much less.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.960
In law school, it's so
easy to get in this mindset

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.450
where your professor is just infallible,

00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:58.290
and they are giving you
the law as it exists

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:01.280
straight from a platonic ideal.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:03.980
But the thing is, your
first-year law subjects,

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:06.340
they seem really hard when you're in it,

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:07.800
but they're really not.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:09.760
When you start practicing, you realize

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.580
just how oversimplified everything is.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.560
And different law professors
have completely different ways

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:18.570
of communicating those
subjects to students,

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:22.960
and there is no universal
conception of the law,

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.960
so it's not like your
professor can give you the law

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.850
as given by Moses and
the tablets or whatever.

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:33.030
I would've worried much less

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.970
about when the professor
said conflicting things,

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:39.940
when the professor said confusing things.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:43.190
A lot of what you learn in
law shool is just confusing.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:46.570
It's complicated when you're
in it for the first time,

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.560
and you have no frame of reference.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:49.890
I wouldn't really have worried

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.760
when the professor said things

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.300
that seemed to contradict each other

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:55.730
or said some conception of the law

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:57.453
that seemed confusing at the time.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.900
Law professors often make things worse.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:04.080
Sometimes they do things
to intentionally obfuscate

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:08.010
so that they can generate
conversation in the class.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.380
Remember that the Socratic method exists

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:11.950
not for the benefit of students,

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:13.780
but for the benefit of the professor

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:16.740
to make it interesting for
them when they're lecturing.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:20.460
The Socratic method is not
a good way to teach the law.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:22.460
It's just simply the historical way

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:25.740
that we've used it for 150 years.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:29.260
So, I wouldn't have worried as much

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.760
when the classroom
discussion is confusing.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:33.610
I wouldn't have worried when the professor

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:36.070
seemed to make it even more complicated.

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:38.940
Now I know that almost
everything that is discussed

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:42.093
in a one-L class is just settled law,

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:45.280
especially when you get
into a particular state.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:47.940
Sometimes a state can vary
with a different state

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.130
in terms of law, but for the most part,

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:51.850
all of the questions that you deal with

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:54.890
in a first-year class
are completely settled.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:56.880
It's only at the very extreme margins

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.680
where there's much controversy,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.670
but that's not what
first-year classes test.

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:04.780
If the professor created
more confusion in class,

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:06.720
I simply would've ignored the professor,

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.740
knowing that I could
figure things out later on

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.700
when I went back to my
commercial supplements

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:13.090
and worked on my outline

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:16.430
or if I looked up the cases on Westlaw.

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:19.100
Knowing what I know now about the law,

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:20.800
it would've made life so much easier,

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.050
because I would've
realized that the professor

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.530
is not the best source of the law,

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:28.450
and I could always go back
and get the black letter law

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:31.210
from my outlines and supplements.

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:33.400
And speaking of which, if
you want additional tips

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:36.370
on how to reveal all the
mysteries of law school

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:38.640
and how to crush your
next law school exam,

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.500
click the link below for
our guide on law school.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.150
And please subscribe to our channel.

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:44.670
We come out with new tips on how to

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:46.420
crush law school all the time.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:49.500
And I'll see you next
time here at Legal Eagle.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.550
Please, learn from my mistakes.

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:52.790
There's no reason that
you should go through

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:54.830
the same kind of crap
that I had to go through.

00:10:54.830 --> 00:10:56.890
Started taking (gabbles).

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:59.133
Start taking practice tests, and it is ...

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.763
And it has very little
to do with actual ...

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.380
Very close to campus, all right.

